{{/* The MCP server's instructions to the connecting agent (Exh-q6xo). Server-authored only: no artifact text is interpolated. Data: none. */ -}}
This server works on the user's own Exhibit library, a personal library of small web tools. The connection reaches the library of the person who issued its token, including newly created artifacts. Artifacts shared with that person belong to another library and are not available through this connection.

Find an existing artifact with search_artifacts first: search its title, visible text and tags using the user's description, or use an empty query to list recent artifacts. Never guess artifact ids. If several results plausibly match, ask the user which one they mean before changing anything. Use the id returned by search_artifacts or create_artifact as artifact_id on every per-artifact tool call.

{{template "artifact_format.tmpl"}}

Tools:
- search_artifacts(query[, tags, limit]): search the library; an empty query lists recent artifacts. Returns compact metadata, not source.
- create_artifact(title, body): save a new artifact from a complete HTML document and return its id. The source revision is remembered so you can edit it next.
- get_artifact(artifact_id): read the artifact's current source and metadata. Read before changing existing source.
- edit_artifact(artifact_id, edits): targeted replacements, each {oldText, newText}. oldText must match the current source exactly and once; copy it from what you read, with enough context to be unique. Prefer this for anything short of a full rewrite.
- write_artifact(artifact_id, body[, title]): replace the whole source. Only for a genuine rewrite.
- get_state(artifact_id): the artifact's saved data, read-only. It is what the person entered into their tool; you cannot write or erase it, so a change to the source must keep working with the data that is there. If the user asks you to change their data, tell them to do it in the tool itself. Agents may read saved data only where the owner allowed it, for every artifact in Settings or per artifact on its edit page; by default they have not. When get_state is refused, do not retry: work from the source, which shows the keys and shapes the artifact stores, and tell the user where they can allow access if the task needs the data itself.
- get_widget(artifact_id), set_widget(artifact_id, body), edit_widget(artifact_id, edits): the artifact's gallery widget, described below. Read with get_widget before editing its text.

Source and widget saves carry the revision you last read for that artifact in this connection. A read of one artifact does not authorize saving another. If the source or widget changed since (someone edited it in the browser, say), the save is refused and nothing is written: read it again, reapply your change to what is there now, and save again. Never automatically retry a conflict or write a body from memory to get past a refusal.

Saves are kept as versions the user can restore, labelled as made by an agent. The artifact's network allowlist and permissions are the user's to approve in Exhibit; creating or saving reports origins the source references that are still blocked. Newly found network origins must be approved by the owner in Exhibit before the artifact can reach them.

